  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Ice dams form when attic heat melts roof snow, and the water refreezes at colder eaves. In Utah's heavy snowfall and cold winter swings, you can stop them with simple short-term habits and long-term home upgrades.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "simple short-term habits and long-term home upgrades"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1,
          2
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Short-Term Prevention & Management"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Rake the snow: Use a long-handled roof rake from the ground to pull the lower 2 to 3 feet of snow off your roof after a heavy storm.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Keep gutters clear: Remove leaves, debris, and hanging icicles before winter so melted snow can drain freely.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Melt channels safely: If an ice dam starts to form, fill a nylon stocking with calcium chloride and lay it vertically across the dam to melt a channel\u2014avoid using rock salt, which ruins shingles and metal.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Install heat cables: Many local homeowners use self-regulating heat cables (available through installers like Wizard Rain Gutters or RGS Exteriors ) run in a zigzag pattern along the eaves and gutters to keep paths open.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              5,
              6,
              7
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Long-Term Roof & Attic Upgrades"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Seal air leaks: Block warm air from escaping your living space into the attic by sealing bypasses around attic hatches, chimney chases, and recessed lights.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              4,
              8
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Boost insulation: Add insulation to your attic floor (aim for at least R-30 to R-38) to keep the roof deck cold and stop snow from melting unevenly.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Improve ventilation: Ensure soffit (eave) and ridge vents are clear so cool outside air circulates uniformly under the entire roof deck.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Upgrade the eave barrier: When replacing your roof, make sure a waterproof self-adhering ice-and-water shield membrane is installed along all eaves and valleys.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you'd like, let me know:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Is your home experiencing active leaking right now?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Do you have vaulted ceilings or a standard attic?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can give you more specific safety steps or local contractor options."
      }
